```apex
public class AccountRatingService {
    @future
    public static void recalculate(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // Aggregate open opportunities per account. One row per account with at least one open opp.
        Map<Id, AggregateResult> openOppAggs = new Map<Id, AggregateResult>();
        for (AggregateResult agg : [
            SELECT AccountId, MAX(Amount) maxAmount
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE AccountId IN :accountIds
              AND IsClosed = false
            GROUP BY AccountId
        ]) {
            openOppAggs.put((Id) agg.get('AccountId'), agg);
        }

        List<Account> accountsToUpdate = new List<Account>();
        for (Account acc : [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds FOR UPDATE]) {
            AggregateResult agg = openOppAggs.get(acc.Id);
            String rating = 'Cold';

            if (agg != null) {
                Decimal maxAmount = (Decimal) agg.get('maxAmount');
                rating = (maxAmount != null && maxAmount >= 100000) ? 'Hot' : 'Warm';
            }

            if (acc.Rating != rating) {
                acc.Rating = rating;
                accountsToUpdate.add(acc);
            }
        }

        if (!accountsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update accountsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

I did this differently from the suggested one-future-call-per-opportunity approach:

1. **I use one `@future` call per trigger execution with a `Set<Id>` of affected accounts, not one call per opportunity.** A transaction can make at most 50 `@future` calls. A CPQ batch of 200 opportunities would exceed that limit if we called `recalculate(opp.AccountId)` for every opportunity. Collecting unique account IDs also avoids recalculating the same account multiple times when multiple opportunities in the batch belong to it.

2. **I use `IsClosed = false` to define “open” rather than hardcoding stage names.** `IsClosed` is driven by the opportunity stage and is org-independent, so it correctly handles customized stage names.

3. **I use an aggregate query (`MAX(Amount) GROUP BY AccountId`)** so the future method does not pull every open opportunity into memory. If an account has any open opportunity with `Amount >= 100000`, the `MAX` will be `>= 100000` and the rating becomes `Hot`; otherwise it becomes `Warm`. No open opportunities means no aggregate row and the rating becomes `Cold`.

4. **I also recalculate the old account when `AccountId` changes on an opportunity.** Without that, the old account could keep a stale rating. If you want to keep strictly to the requested trigger events, you can remove that block.

