public with sharing class LineItemDiscountHandler {
    public static void applyStandardDiscount(List<OpportunityLineItem> newLines) {
        Set<Id> productIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null && line.Product2Id != null) {
                productIds.add(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }

        if (productIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        Map<Id, Product2> productsById = new Map<Id, Product2>(
            [SELECT Id, Family FROM Product2 WHERE Id IN :productIds]
        );

        Map<Id, Decimal> discountByProductId = new Map<Id, Decimal>();
        for (Product2 product : productsById.values()) {
            discountByProductId.put(
                product.Id,
                PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily(product.Family)
            );
        }

        for (OpportunityLineItem line : newLines) {
            if (line.Discount == null
                    && line.Product2Id != null
                    && discountByProductId.containsKey(line.Product2Id)) {
                line.Discount = discountByProductId.get(line.Product2Id);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

I changed the approach from calling `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor(line.Product2Id)` for every line to querying `Product2` once and calling `PricingRules.standardDiscountForFamily`. `PricingRules.standardDiscountFor` performs a SOQL query every time it is called; with up to 200 lines per opportunity, that would exceed the 100 SOQL limit. The discount rules still live in `PricingRules`, so nothing is copied into the handler.

