public with sharing class LeadContactMatcher {
    public static List<Id> matchByEmail(List<Lead> leads, List<Contact> contacts) {
        Map<String, Id> firstContactIdByEmail = new Map<String, Id>();
        if (contacts != null) {
            for (Contact contact : contacts) {
                if (contact == null) {
                    continue;
                }
                String email = normalizeEmail(contact.Email);
                if (email != null && !firstContactIdByEmail.containsKey(email)) {
                    firstContactIdByEmail.put(email, contact.Id);
                }
            }
        }

        List<Id> matchedContactIds = new List<Id>();
        if (leads != null) {
            for (Lead lead : leads) {
                if (lead == null) {
                    matchedContactIds.add(null);
                    continue;
                }
                String email = normalizeEmail(lead.Email);
                matchedContactIds.add(email == null ? null : firstContactIdByEmail.get(email));
            }
        }
        return matchedContactIds;
    }

    private static String normalizeEmail(String email) {
        if (email == null) {
            return null;
        }
        String normalized = email.trim().toLowerCase();
        return normalized == '' ? null : normalized;
    }
}
```

I’d use this Map-based approach instead of a nested loop. It is O(n) and still readable, avoids up to 9 million comparisons inside the import transaction, and naturally handles the “first contact wins” rule.

